        "id": "p-ifever-science",
        "title": "Passage 3 \u2014 Communication in Science",
        "body": "SECTION 3 — Communication in Science\n\nA  Science plays an increasingly significant role in people's lives, making the faithful communication of scientific developments more important than ever. Yet communication between scientists and the general public is plagued by difficulties. First, there is the esoteric nature of modern research, which often deals with phenomena far removed from daily human experience. Second, scientists frequently employ ambiguous language or everyday colloquial words that carry precise, specialized definitions. Third, because research is continually evolving, scientists themselves often only partially understand the full implications of ongoing discoveries in fields such as global warming, epidemiology, and cosmology.\n\nB  The word 'relativity' is a quintessential example of terminology that is intrinsically misleading to the layperson. When Albert Einstein introduced his theories, the popular press and general public seized on the phrase 'everything is relative', interpreting it as an endorsement of moral or perceptual relativism: the idea that there is no absolute truth and that reality is merely a matter of subjective perspective. To many people, Einstein appeared to have dismantled the objective foundations of the physical universe.\n\nC  In truth, Einstein's objective was the exact opposite. His theory of relativity aims to formulate an invariant description of physical phenomena — principles of nature that remain identical for every observer regardless of their motion or coordinate system. Rather than dissolving absolutes, the theory establishes that the speed of light in a vacuum is universally constant and that the fundamental laws of physics are invariant across all inertial frames. Thus, what is variable is merely the observer's relative measurement of space and time, which adjust in such a way that the laws themselves remain immutable.\n\nD  The choice of colloquial terminology can therefore distort profound ideas. When scientists assign precise, technical meanings to everyday words such as 'relativity', 'uncertainty', or 'chaos', the general public naturally interprets them in common everyday terms. Poor word choices — ambiguous labels borrowed from ordinary speech — confuse lay audiences and generate widespread misconceptions that persist for decades in textbooks and journalism.\n\nE  The consequences extend beyond physics. In debates over global warming, epidemiology, and cosmology, partial understanding among researchers themselves compounds the problem: ongoing discoveries revise models faster than curricula can absorb them. Journalists, working under deadline pressure, often compress nuanced findings into slogans. The result is a public that doubts settled measurements while over-trusting preliminary claims.\n\nF  Remedies are practical rather than philosophical. Research groups increasingly publish plain-language summaries alongside technical papers, define colloquial terminology explicitly before using it, and train spokespeople to state what each observer will measure rather than what 'is'. Such invariant descriptions — framed around the observer and the measurement — preserve accuracy without surrendering precision.\n\nG  Einstein's groundbreaking work is formally known as the theory of general relativity. Its lesson for communication endures: name ideas carefully, because everyday word choices shape public reason. When the language is exact, the science can be grasped; when it is casual, even genius is misread as relativism."
