        "title": "Passage 1 \u2014 Science & Nature (Rockets, Dung Beetles & Caloric)",
        "body": "Passage 1 — Science & Nature\n\nPart A: The History of Rockets\nThe invention of rockets is linked inextricably with the invention of 'black powder'. Most historians of technology credit the Chinese with its discovery, compounding saltpetre, charcoal and sulphur around the tenth century. By the thirteenth century, powder-propelled fire arrows had become rather common. The Chinese relied on this to produce incendiary projectiles, explosive grenades and cannons. One weapon was the 'basket of fire' (arrows like flying leopards), fired from an octagonal basket with a range of 400 paces. Another was the 'arrow as a flying sabre', fired from crossbows with iron counterweights for stability. At a similar time, the Arabs developed the 'egg which moves and burns' using two rockets attached to a tail. In the late 18th century, Indian rocketeers successfully used iron envelope rockets with 3m bamboo sticks against the British. The British later developed stout iron cylinder barrage rockets, while Americans built propped-up launchers used against Mexico. In short: black powder and fire arrows trace to the Chinese; war rockets to the Indians; the dedicated launcher to the Americans.\n\nPart B: Dung Beetles in Pasture Ecology\nIntroducing dung beetles into a pasture is a simple process: approximately 1,500 beetles are released into fresh cow pats. The beetles immediately burrow beneath the pats, sheltering from predators like birds and foxes. Large French species excavate sausage-shaped brood chambers up to 30 cm below the surface. Smaller Spanish species bury dung in chambers hanging like fruit from tree branches. South African species dig narrow 20 cm tunnels or roll dung balls away to plant bases. For maximum burial in spring, summer and autumn, farmers combine species across climates. The temperate-climate Spanish species becomes active in early spring and produces two to five generations annually, complementing the slower French species. The sub-tropical South African ball roller works alongside the South African tunneling species, each covering different soils and seasons.\n\nPart C: Caloric Restriction Mimetic\nNo treatment today slows human aging, but caloric restriction (low-calorie, nutritionally balanced diets) increases longevity in animals. Researchers seek a caloric-restriction mimetic: a pill replicating eating less without eating less. The candidate 2DG interferes with how cells process glucose: less glucose is processed and less ATP is synthesized. Theory 1 holds that quieter ATP machinery emits fewer free radicals, limiting cellular damage linked to cancer and aging. Theory 2 holds that scarce glucose signals cells to shift into preservation mode, emphasizing maintenance of the organism over growth and reproduction because food is scarce.\n\nPart D: A Note on Marie Curie\nMarie Curie, born Maria Sklodowska, shared the 1903 Nobel Prize in Physics with Pierre Curie and Henri Becquerel, then won the 1911 Nobel Prize in Chemistry alone. Her husband was therefore a joint winner of only her first Nobel Prize, not both — a detail that matters for precise reading."
