        "id": "p-off-s2",
        "title": "Passage 2 \u2014 History, Cities & Society (Curie, Traffic & Jewel House)",
        "body": "Passage 2 — History, Cities & Society\n\nPart A: Marie Curie's Early Life\nMarie Curie, born Maria Sklodowska in Poland in 1867, won the 1903 Nobel Prize in Physics and 1911 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Endowed with prodigious memory, she graduated secondary school with a gold medal at 16. To fund her sister Bronia's medical studies in Paris, she worked as a governess on the agreement that Bronia would later finance Marie's university education. In 1891, Marie enrolled at the Sorbonne, surviving on bread, butter, and tea while topping examinations in physical and mathematical sciences before meeting Pierre Curie in 1894. The passage does not state when she first became interested in science as a child.\n\nPart B: The Physics of Highway Traffic Flow\nIn Stuttgart, theoretical physicists Dirk Helbing and Boris Kerner modeled vehicular highway traffic using equations of gas dynamics. Though drivers slow to avoid collisions unlike molecules, the flowing-gas model accurately predicts spontaneous traffic jams without external causes. Section C presents this transfer of a concept from one field (gas physics) to another (traffic). Slight local speed or distance fluctuations trigger persistent systemic breakdowns akin to phase transitions in chaos theory. Section D proposes to take control by timing each vehicle's highway entry to coincide with temporary density drops, while noting areas of doubt among civil engineers who prefer physical road expansion. Section E examines the impact of driver behaviour: Professor James Banks notes aggressive lane changes by frustrated drivers also equalize speeds across lanes.\n\nPart C: The Jewel House & Elizabethan Science\nDeborah Harkness's study, The Jewel House, examines 16th-century London's scientific community. Rather than searching for isolated heroic geniuses, Harkness began by understanding what the terms 'science' and 'scientist' meant in that period. She investigated everyday meanings of science, documenting apothecary shops, botanical gardens, and alchemical workshops on Lime Street. Her reconstruction was new because she examined how methods and practices evolved over time within textual communities. Elizabethan London scientists were innovative because they used old ways for new purposes: compiling empirical knowledge through active practical experimentation rather than relying solely on classical authority.\n\nPart D: Older Workers in the Labour Market\nResearch on age and work finds older employees show sound judgment and productivity, with education standards often better than today's young school-leavers. Their advantages include an ability to predict trouble before it escalates and skill in maintaining personal relationships at work. By contrast, younger workers' disadvantages are well documented: they frequently do not stay long in one post, offering poor returns on training, and are often not as well educated as previous cohorts. Firms that retain older staff report conscientious performance and fewer careless accidents."
