        "title": "Passage 3 \u2014 Work, Science & Evolution (Older Workers, Radium & Birds)",
        "body": "Passage 3 — Work, Science & Evolution\n\nPart A: Older Workers & Bridge Jobs\nWhile heavy physical endurance declines with age, older workers often excel in diplomacy, crisis avoidance, and institutional judgment. The writer suggests companies could consider abolishing pay schemes based on age, since seniority pay makes older staff look costly despite strong productivity. When companies seek cost reductions, early retirement schemes are frequently deployed. IBM Belgium established 'Skill Team' to re-employ retirees aged 55-60 at 88% salary for 58% working time — an example of a company which allows expertise to be put to use rather than discarded. In the United States, nearly half of full-time middle-aged employees transition to part-time 'bridge jobs'. According to the writer, 'bridge' jobs appeal to distinct groups: those who must keep earning and those who work for fulfillment. David Storey's study of enterprise found that older people are good at running businesses, contradicting stereotypes about entrepreneurial age.\n\nPart B: Radium Isolation & Scientific Legacy\nFollowing Henri Becquerel's 1896 discovery of uranium radioactivity, Marie Curie decided to test whether other elements shared the property and found the element thorium did. Examining pitchblende ore, a mineral whose activity exceeded pure uranium, Marie and Pierre isolated polonium and radium. With chemist Andre-Louis Debierne, Marie isolated pure metallic radium, earning her doctorate and a second Nobel Prize in 1911 for work on the element radium. After Pierre's tragic death in 1906, Marie succeeded his professorship at the Sorbonne as the university's first female professor. During World War I, Marie and her daughter Irene developed mobile X-radiography units for wounded soldiers. Marie saw the importance of collecting radioactive material both for research and for cases of illness. Stocks of radon held in Paris later contributed to the discovery of the neutron and of artificial radioactivity.\n\nPart C: Origins of Birds & Dinosaur Ancestry\nFollowing Willi Hennig's phylogenetic cladistics, evolutionary biologists trace relationships through shared novel characteristics; in cladistics, the similarities between characteristics are of major importance. In the 1860s, Thomas Huxley identified identical lower leg and ankle bone structures in meat-eating theropod dinosaurs and common quail, proposing birds descended from dinosaurs. Huxley studied a dinosaur belonging to the group called theropods. Although Gerhard Heilmann challenged this in 1916 by claiming dinosaurs lacked collarbones — the bones that fuse into avian furculae — noting the apparent absence of collarbones in dinosaurs, John Ostrom discovered 22 shared skeletal traits in the late 1960s. Feduccia and Martin dissent, believing the ancestor was an early reptile rather than a theropod. The discovery of feathered fossil creatures like Archaeopteryx solidified the evolutionary bridge linking theropods to modern birds."
